  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu Apr 16 2026\n\n...FRIDAY...\nSubtropical jet flow increases on Friday, with a 996mb surface \nlow across the High Plains allowing for breezy conditions across\nSouthern New Mexico and Far West Texas. West winds 15 to 25 mph\nFriday afternoon, with gusts up to 40 mph mid-afternoon. Patchy\nblowing dust is possible, especially around known sensitive areas\nsuch as the Lordsburg Playa, portions of Luna County, and the dust\nsources in northern Chihuahua. Conditions may be hazy again across\nEl Paso.\n\n...THIS WEEKEND...\nCold front spilling down the Southern High Plains Friday night \nwill reach Southern New Mexico early Saturday morning. Winds will \nshift out of the east over the weekend, leading to a cooler \nweekend (Lowland highs mid 70s Saturday, lower 70s Sunday). Winds \ndo look gusty especially along mountain west slopes Saturday \nnight, which would include west El Paso. More blowing dust will be\npossible around the Lordsburg Playa along I-10 on Sunday.\n\nMoisture return up the Rio Grande valley will increase dewpoints\nand push precipitable water back up to around 0.8\" Sunday night.\nRain showers will be possible across El Paso/Hudspeth/Otero \nCounties Sunday night into Monday, though any rain that does fall \nwill be very light.\n\n...MONDAY AND TUESDAY...\nWarming trend early next week, with lowland highs back into the \nmid 80s by Tuesday afternoon. Ridging flow aloft will also keep \nwind speeds down over the timeframe, so minimal weather threats. \nMarginal moisture sticks around on Monday, which could lead to \nvery spotty rain over the Sacramento Mountains, otherwise the rest\nof the region will remain dry. \n\n...WEDNESDAY AND THURSDAY...\nStrengthening southwest flow ahead of a Pacific low will bring \nmore breezy to windy conditions midweek. We may need Wind \nHeadlines depending on the eventual evolution and timing of this \nsystem. Nevertheless, confidence is high in a warm, dry, and \nbreezy weather pattern.",
